    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. My concern is that these aren't necessarily large-scale embezzlement attempts, just these tiny, annoying amounts—like €12 here or €15 there—that pop up at odd intervals. It feels like they are almost \"hiding\" in the noise. \n\nIf I wanted to narrow down whether this is just poor documentation or something more intentional, how should I approach the data sampling? I don't want to spend weeks looking at every single line item.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To sample the data and determine the scope of the issue, start by selecting a random subset of the transactions (e.g., 20-50% of the total). Analyze this small sample to identify patterns, inconsistencies, or correlations between codes and employees. Look for instances of transactions that are significantly out of line with the company's normal procurement cycles or have unusual payment methods. Additionally, check if any transactions coincide with similar or identical entries from other employees or departments.",
