    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The random sampling helps, but the problem is that the entries are so sporadic and small that even in a large sample, they don't always show a clear 'smoking gun' pattern. They just seem to manifest out of nowhere, almost like they're intentionally slipping through the cracks of our standard oversight. \n\nIs there a way to look for 'clumping' or certain temporal patterns that might indicate someone is intentionally exploiting the threshold of what we typically flag? I'm looking for a way to track these little irregularities more effectively.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To identify clumping or temporal patterns, analyze the frequency and timing of the discrepancies. Look for instances where multiple transactions occur within a short period, such as a monthly or quarterly cluster. You can also use time-series analysis or time-distance analysis to calculate the frequency and timing of these sporadic occurrences. For example, identify if transactions are happening during the same time each quarter, or if there's a specific 10-day window when they tend to appear.",
